<GuideLayout 
  title="Sourcing Red Flags: 5 Warnings for Professional Activewear Buyers"
  type="SOURCING"
  date="June 30, 2026"
  readTime="9 min read"
>
  <p>Vetting a new manufacturing partner is the most critical step for any sourcing manager. While many suppliers show beautiful photos, the reality of mass production can be different. Here are 5 red flags every professional buyer must watch for.</p>
  
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Vague Fabric Specifications:</strong> If a supplier cannot provide the GSM, fiber blend, or shrinkage rate of a fabric, they are likely not the direct manufacturer.</li>
    <li><strong>Inconsistent Sample Quality:</strong> A sample that looks great but lacks professional finishing (like reinforced seams) is a sign of poor quality control in bulk production.</li>
    <li><strong>Hidden Lead Time Buffers:</strong> Beware of "instant" shipping claims. Professional manufacturing, even for ready-stock with logos, requires a minimum of 7-10 days for proper QC.</li>
    <li><strong>Lack of Export Documentation:</strong> For international shipping (especially via hubs like Miami), precise HS codes and certificates of origin are essential to avoid customs delays.</li>
    <li><strong>Poor Communication Speed:</strong> B2B manufacturing is a partnership. If a supplier takes 48 hours to reply to a simple query, your production schedule will be at risk.</li>
  </ul>
  
  <p>At FITCLO, we pride ourselves on transparency, technical precision, and rapid communication—the foundations of a successful B2B relationship.</p>
</GuideLayout>
